Project: js-xss
Repository: github.com/leizongmin/js-xss
Issue Addressed: #284 — How to see what tags are removed?
My Pull Request: feature/filter-xss-with-result

**What is this project?**
`js-xss` is a JavaScript library with over 5,300 GitHub stars used by developers worldwide to sanitize untrusted HTML. Its job is to prevent Cross-Site Scripting attacks, commonly called XSS attacks.
An XSS attack happens when a malicious user submits dangerous HTML or JavaScript into a website — for example typing `<script>stealYourPassword()</script>` into a comment box. If the website displays that input without cleaning it first, the script runs in every other user's browser and can steal session tokens, redirect users, or perform actions on their behalf.
js-xss solves this by maintaining a whitelist — a list of HTML tags and attributes that are considered safe, such as `<p>`, `<a href="">`, and `<img src="">`. Anything not on that whitelist gets stripped out before the HTML is shown to users. The library is used in production applications handling real user data, which is why correctness and reliability matter deeply.

**What was the problem?*
When `js-xss` cleans a piece of HTML, it gives back only the sanitized output string. For example:

```
Input:   <script>alert("xss")</script><a href="#" onclick="evil()">click</a>
Output:  &lt;script&gt;alert("xss")&lt;/script&gt;<a href="#">click</a>
```
The library cleaned it — but it told you nothing about what it removed. The `onclick` attribute is gone. The `<script>` tag is escaped. But from the output alone, you can't tell what was stripped or why.
This was the pain point reported in issue #284. A developer needed to build a validation error system — something that could tell end users "your input contained these forbidden elements." The library had no built-in way to provide that information. The only workaround was to manually wire up internal callback hooks called `onIgnoreTag` and `onIgnoreTagAttr` every single time you used the library, which was repetitive, undocumented for this use case, and easy to get wrong.

**What did I do?**
I studied the entire source code of the library — `xss.js`, `index.js`, `default.js`, `parser.js`, and `util.js` — to understand exactly how the filtering pipeline works from the moment HTML enters the function to the moment clean HTML comes out.
I identified that the library already fires internal hook functions at the exact moment each tag or attribute is removed. These hooks exist to allow customization, but they weren't being used to collect removal data in any structured way. The solution was to intercept those hooks, record what they see, and expose the collected data alongside the normal output.
I added a new function called `filterXSSWithResult(html, options)` to `lib/index.js.` This function:

1. Creates an empty list called `removed` before filtering begins
2. Makes a safe copy of the user's options so their original object is never modified
3. Saves any hook functions the user may have already provided
4. Installs its own versions of `onIgnoreTag` and `onIgnoreTagAttr` that record each removal into the `removed` list, then call the user's original hooks afterward so nothing breaks
5. Runs the existing `filterXSS()` function with these modified options
6. Returns both the clean HTML and the populated `removed` list together

The result is that users can now do this in a single call:
``` javascript
var result = xss.filterXSSWithResult(
  '<script>alert("xss")</script><a href="#" onclick="evil()">click</a>'
);

console.log(result.html);
// &lt;script&gt;alert("xss")&lt;/script&gt;<a href="#">click</a>

console.log(result.removed);
// [
//   { type: "tag",  tag: "script", html: "<script>",  isClosing: false },
//   { type: "tag",  tag: "script", html: "</script>", isClosing: true  },
//   { type: "attr", tag: "a",      attr: "onclick",   value: "evil()" }
// ]
```
I also wrote a test file with four test cases covering: dangerous tags being recorded, dangerous attributes being recorded, clean HTML producing an empty removed array, and mixed input containing both bad tags and bad attributes simultaneously.


